# 6. QUICK SAVE — THE MOST IMPORTANT WORKFLOW

Saving a new reference should take only a few seconds.

Create a clearly visible button labeled "+ Add Inspiration" or "Quick Save".

Clicking it should open a clean modal or dedicated form with two options:

## Option A: Upload an image

Allow me to:

- Choose an image from my computer.
- Drag and drop an image into the upload area.
- See an image preview before saving.
- Enter an optional title.
- Add optional personal notes.
- Assign optional collections and tags.
- Save the reference.

Support common image formats such as PNG, JPEG, and WebP.

Validate file types and file sizes.

Provide upload progress, clear errors, and a retry option when appropriate.

Preserve the original uploaded image whenever practical. If generating thumbnails or optimized copies, retain access to the original.

Do not pretend an image was uploaded when it has not been stored successfully.

## Option B: Save a website URL

Allow me to paste a website URL.

When possible, retrieve useful information such as:

- Page title.
- Website domain.
- Page description.
- Preview image.
- Original URL.

Use safe server-side metadata retrieval or an appropriate external service.

Do not assume every website allows automated access. Some websites block previews, require authentication, or prevent scraping.

If metadata cannot be retrieved, the reference must still be saveable.

Provide an optional manual title, image upload, and personal notes.

Always preserve the original URL exactly as submitted after appropriate URL validation and normalization decisions.

Prevent server-side request forgery if the backend fetches external URLs. Validate protocols, block localhost and private network destinations, restrict redirects, and apply safe response-size and timeout limits.

Do not bypass website restrictions or attempt to access protected content.

## Option C: Save an image and URL together

Support saving a screenshot alongside its source URL.

This should be a first-class workflow, not an afterthought.

## Quick Save design principles

- Minimal fields.
- Optional title and notes.
- No mandatory AI analysis.
- No unnecessary multi-step wizard.
- Clear success and error feedback.
- No accidental loss of form data.
- Save to persistent storage before showing success.

After saving, return me to my library or allow me to continue adding references.

